ey can connect wearable  devices such as  fitbit garmin and apple watch they can  answer surveys for points  and then these points can be redeemed or  donated  uh over on the enterprise side we  partner with leading healthcare  companies to understand health and  disease outside the clinic walls  with our achievement platform we enable  our members to participate in  groundbreaking health research  so for the past three flu seasons  evadation has deployed an opt-in flu  symptom experience monitoring program  so here's an overview of the in-app flow  where members are able to share their  flu symptoms directly in the achievement  app  using the permission survey responses  along with sensor data from wearables  like fitbit garmin and apple watch we  can train models for predicting flu  symptoms  so last flu season we adapted one of our  flu and kobit 19 detection research  models and deployed it within the  achievement app to provide daily flu  daily predictions of flu symptoms to our  members  we ran a small scale poc in production  with several thousand enrolled members  where we computed features from wearable  activity data  so this included  features around activity heart rate and  sleep  then we cast daily predictions with an  activity-based machine learning model  and finally we sent out notifications to  our achievement members  where the model had predicted positive  flu symptoms  so the poc was a big success overall but  there are a lot of headaches for the  data science team  and  yeah i want to walk through like  what are the  many challenges that we face when  productionizing machine learning  pipelines  from having to deal with  feature drift and data leakage to  latency requirements like there's a lot  going on  in this talk we're going to focus more  on the software dev and engineering side  of things  so data scientists love using jupiter  notebooks for the fast iteration and  development  but there are issues with using them in  prod  so there's two main options one is we  can use the notebooks in production  or the second option is we  take the  development notebooks and we translate  them to a non-notebook code base  if we go with option one  using the jupyter notebooks in  production  because they're stored in json they've  historically been a real pain to version  control and code review  this makes standard development  workflows cumbersome and the entire  development review process really gets  bogged down  using notebooks also encourage the use  of monolithic notebook files to run the  pipeline  these like mega files are really  difficult to update and iterate upon  testing and monitoring them is really  manual and slow  and debugging requires re-running the  whole pipeline file so even if it's just  a small  part of the pipeline that needs to be  debugged  um you have to rerun the whole thing and  this is really cumbersome and slows down  iteration  additionally trying to use notebooks in  production often ends up involving a  hybrid use of notebooks with python  modules where your notebooks are calling  to python utilities  so it's actually kind of hard to solely  use notebooks in production  so this brings us to option two  which is to translate the notebooks to a  non-notebook production code base  the issues with this are it's time  consuming and it adds complexity  high overhead to  adding testing and documentation  frameworks into your code base  means that  there's a high burden uh to integrating  them and  then tests and docs risk becoming an  afterthought  uh source code tests and docs often end  up living in different files so this  also increases maintenance costs makes  it more likely that these pieces will  quickly become out of sync  finally the translation process  increases the distance between  development and production  which increases the likelihood that the  model behavior in development won't  match production  so this brings us to plumer  we did a comparative analysis of open  source pipeline orchestration tools and  we picked plumer because  it's easy to get started with  easy and accessible for data scientists  across the orb to adopt  and it supports both python and r which  is great because we have both r and  python data scientists in our org we  wanted to enable everyone to adopt  workflow management  additionally polymer supports are  orchestrating and developing with  jupiter notebooks  so with plumer we're able to rapidly  iterate explore and develop directly  using jupiter notebooks  it enables modularization so we can  easily break the pipeline up into  separate tasks  and it really saves us a lot of time  with incremental builds so we can speed  up the debugging process  so diving into the first point uh  rapidly iterate explore and develop with  jupiter notebooks  so polymer uses jupytex to seamlessly  convert between  python and ipymb files  and this means that we can continue  working and developing with jupiter so  we're able to reduce conversion costs  it enables faster development and  iteration  and it allows us to create living log  files so these are executed notebooks  outputted from runs  where we can easily integrate docs  metrics plots  source code  anything that you could include in a  jupyter notebook  all into one accessible file  and this really makes developing and  debugging the pipeline much more  accessible for data scientists  uh plumer also stores source code for  tasks as python files uh which makes  things really easy for us to version  control and code review  and  uh it allows team members to use their  preferred ide so for certain development  tasks jupiter lab might be better to use  something like vs code might be better  for others  and this way you get the best of both  worlds  so the second piece is plumer enables  modularization uh so we can easily break  the pipeline up into separate tests  bloomer lets you set up and manage the  dag definition including the individual  tasks their products so 