 reviewing  notebooks really easy  so here we have a testimony in the wild  from a data scientist that was  onboarding to this project  and they were just so impressed by how  easy nbw was making development with  jupiter notebooks  and then on this slide we have an  overview of  uh what using mbdev looks like so  we have the jupiter notebook  in the top left and this is where we  have our source code examples tests and  dots  these notebooks really let you tell a  story with the code you're developing  and let you keep all this useful content  in in one central place  nbdev then converts this notebook into a  python module and automatically sets up  the ci to make sure that your package is  building correctly  uh it's running the tests that you  defined in your notebooks since doing  that in parallel  and it creates the html pages for your  docs  so on the  right side we have an example of one of  these html pages  and  you can see it's automatically  created organized and searchable  documentation  complete with a table of contents  api references  for your source code  examples and tests that you had defined  in your notebook  docs strings and explanations  and even links to source code  uh so here's another example of a page  that's been automatically generated  by nbdev from a jupiter notebook  this page serves as the readme for the  repo  so new  contributors can quickly get up to speed  by looking through the documentation on  how to contribute  uh they can get an overview of the repo  and what the different files and  directories are for  they can see an automatically generated  plot of the plumer dag  outlining all the tasks in the pipeline  and since this is all a jupiter notebook  behind the scenes it's really trivial to  add things like plots or images or mark  down to your documentation  and so now that setting up documentation  is so easy  we can more easily ensure that our docs  are up to date and  really make it easy for our data  scientists to onboard to new  projects  so what are the key takeaways that i  hope you'll take away from this talk  jupiter is a production ready platform  with mbdev and plumer we developed a  workflow that allows us to produce  maintainable robust production-ready  machine learning pipelines directly from  jupiter  and this flu season we're doing it again  so we're running  uh we're currently running in production  to send personalized daily flu  predictions to hundreds of thousands of  achievement members  and this was really enabled with plumer  and mb devs plumer which allows us to  develop and orchestrate our pipelines  and lets us rapidly iterate explore and  develop with jupiter notebooks it  enables modularization so we can easily  break up pipelines into separate tasks  and it saves time with incremental  builds and speeds up debugging  and then  we're able to create python  projects with nbdev  so we can develop software with jupyter  notebooks we can keep all of our source  code tests and docs in one place  and it automatically sets up testing  building docs and ci for us  so here are some resources you may find  useful um the  developers of bloomer and nbdev have  great tutorials  uh blog posts and documentation on their  websites  if you're interested in learning more  about these tools i highly recommend  checking those out  and  if you're interested in learning more  about the work and research we do at  edition please feel free to reach out  and check out our website  um so thanks so much for having me and  happy to take any questions  all right thank you very much i really  appreciate  this  talk philip so i'm going to review  i think  eduardo blancas is mentioning this is an  amazing talk  i'm also reviewing our question and  answers panel  well for our participants does anybody  have any question  all right thanks a lot  dan we have one question from dan  and he's asking asking are users of are  able to contribute to the in development  work  yeah so um  the  this 